Original commit body from 036bab4894658d4693ed153d17c092c86ee6f99e:

`c := &http.Client{}; c.Do(req)` — an outbound HTTP request with a
possibly-tainted URL — produced no I/O boundary in ANY spelling. Not
"typed one way and not another": there was no way to write it in Go
that worked. `Do`/`Get` are in go.yaml's ambiguous_names BY DESIGN, so
receiver typing is the only thing that can reach those rows, and the
receiver never carried a type.

A DRIFTED COPY, not a missing feature. `_type_identifier_from_node`
already handles `qualified_type` and its docstring says returning the
full `http.Client` is "critical for IO boundary detection".
`_type_from_rhs` re-implemented a narrower `type_identifier`-only
version inline, so two spellings of one declaration disagreed:

    var client http.Client     -> "http.Client"   (worked)
    client := http.Client{}    -> ""              (broken)
    client := &http.Client{}   -> ""              (broken)

The fix routes the copy through the existing predicate. The parity test
pins AGREEMENT across the three spellings rather than pinning the new
path to a new expectation, so a fourth spelling must join or fail.

Root cause worth carrying to other languages: tree-sitter spells a
package-qualified name by SYNTACTIC POSITION — selector_expression in
expression position, qualified_type in type position. Handling one
silently drops the other. The same distinction produced a bug in the
sizing instrument, caught by its positive control.

MEASURED, both directions, 6 Go repos, whole diff in two processes:
call-edge count IDENTICAL (76,689 = 76,689), +25 I/O boundaries tagged,
net +76 intra-repo dsts, -746 `external` placeholder slots. Positive
control names the edge under test (PRE go:external Do tags 0, POST
go:net/http Do tags 1) after an earlier version of the control was
found to pass in BOTH arms because http.NewRequest tags regardless.

Read edge-by-edge on caddy, because an aggregate cannot tell a removed
FALSE edge from a removed TRUE one: of 222 distinct edges lost, 79 were
upgraded off the `external` placeholder, ~95 re-spelled onto the
documented repo-relative convention, and 48 were false intra-repo edges
— 17 of them pointing from production code at a symbol defined in a
_test.go file.

The fix needed a second half. Binding the type walks these receivers
into the branch that strips `url.Values` to bare `Values` and looks it
up among the repo's OWN symbols — reviving the alertmanager
13-spurious-in-edges bug the WI-jopar guard closed. The first version
of this fix did exactly that. `_external_package_for_type` is the
discriminator: a type whose package lies outside this go.mod module
cannot be defined by this repo, so the local lookup is skipped. It
ABSTAINS without a module path (in-repo and external are the same shape
there) — a disclosed gap, not a guess.

A downstream guard keyed on a proxy this change inverts. WI-jopar fired
on `import_path_hint is None`, which stood in for "the receiver is a
tracked local" only while qualified composite literals stayed untyped.
It now keys on the receiver directly and terminates with the package it
knows instead of the placeholder.

INV-fazim's producer half closes at the filed line as a side effect:
that guard called Edge.create with no is_resolved=, taking the True
default onto a dst whose kind slot reads `unresolved`. Producer-stage
is_resolved=True falls by 10,319. NOT claimed to change the serialized
map — finalize sub-step 7 re-derives the verdict from the dst node kind
(verified in code). Three sibling sites keep the same omission.

Also: three Go integration tests had never executed (WI-jolif). Their
fixture probed a non-existent method inside a bare `except Exception:
pytest.skip`, so an AttributeError read as "grammar unavailable" on a
machine where it is installed. Repaired, and the three now fail
honestly as xfail(strict=True) — reproduced identically against
unmodified dev, so pre-existing. Both repaired fixtures have no
exception handler at all.

